## Language Server Tools

You have access to language server (LSP) tools that understand code semantically. Use these tools when working with named symbols (functions, types, variables, etc.). If any LSP tool returns an error saying language servers are still indexing, stop and wait for the user to run it again — do not retry immediately.

- Use `find_references` when you need to find all usages of a specific symbol. `find_references` is semantically aware and will not miss references that `grep` cannot find (e.g. calls through trait implementations, dynamic dispatch, or different import styles) while also avoiding false positives from text matches in comments or strings. No `enclosing_scope` is needed — the tool queries all scopes automatically and deduplicates results.
- Use `go_to_definition` when you need to understand what a symbol is, where it is defined, or how it is implemented. This is more precise than `grep` for finding the canonical definition of a symbol. No `enclosing_scope` is needed — the tool queries all scopes automatically and deduplicates results.
- Use `rename_symbol` instead of `edit_file` or `grep`-and-replace when the user asks to rename a symbol. `rename_symbol` performs a semantic rename that updates all references across the project safely, including in imports, type annotations, and string literals where the language server determines a rename is appropriate. If the symbol name appears only once in the file, no `enclosing_scope` is needed. If it appears in multiple scopes, the tool will list each scope with the line text at that occurrence — provide `enclosing_scope` matching the scope you want. The rename will operate on the first matching symbol within the chosen scope.
- Use `get_code_actions` followed by `apply_code_action` when fixing diagnostics. Language servers provide quick fixes and refactorings (e.g. "Add missing import", "Extract to function", "Implement missing trait methods") that are more reliable and complete than manual edits. You must provide a `file_path` and `line` number — no symbol name or `enclosing_scope` needed.
- Each scope segment in `enclosing_scope` is matched if the outline item's full text ends with that segment, so `"Editor"` matches both `"struct Editor"` and `"impl Editor"`, while `"run"` does not match `"fn run_and_wait"`. For example, to rename `new` inside `impl Editor`, use `["Editor"]`. `enclosing_scope` is only needed for `rename_symbol` when the symbol name appears in multiple scopes.
{{/if}}
